## Problem

A range endpoint whose type is narrower than the range element type is stored into the `Range<T>` struct without a widening conversion. `StoreRangeInit` in `Source/Lir.cpp` lowered the endpoint and stored it using the element type directly:

```cpp
const LirReg hiVal = LowerExpr(*e.hi);   // e.g. a uint32 value
EmitStore(hiVal, hiPtr, elemType);       // stored as int (64-bit)
```

When the bound is narrower than the element type (a `uint32` const used in a `Range<int>`), the backend writes the value with a 32-bit store but reads it back with a 64-bit load. The high 32 bits of the bound are never initialized, so they come from leftover stack memory. The garbage bound makes the loop run zero times (high bit set, bound reads as negative) or never terminate (high bits make the bound a large positive value), nondeterministically across runs of the same binary. Literal bounds are unaffected because an integer literal is already typed as the element type.

## Reproduction

```rux
import Std::Io::Print;
import Std::ToString;

const N: uint32 = 624;

func Main() -> int {
    var count: uint32 = 0;
    for i in 1..N {
        count += 1;
    }
    Print(ToString(count));   // expected 623; prints 0 or hangs
    Print("\n");
    return 0;
}
```

The same loop written with a literal bound (`1..624`) always prints `623`.

## Fix

Cast each range endpoint to the element type before storing it, using the existing `EmitCastIfNeeded` helper (the same pattern assignment lowering already uses). The cast loads the value with its own narrow type, which zero- or sign-extends it to the full width, and then stores the full field. All in `Source/Lir.cpp`.

## Tests

Extends `Tests/For` with const exclusive, const inclusive, and const lower-and-upper range bounds. The new cases hang on the unpatched compiler and pass with the fix. Full suite passes.

Fixes #161.

